WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.840
That was you, Hédi Fried, welcome.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:07.820
Thank you.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.340
You have ...

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.600
You have spoken ...

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.240
... with and to you people for 30 years.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.300
Yes.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.860
... about holocaust.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.920
Have ...

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:34.180
And you've now collected their most common, and the most important, questions from them in a book.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:34.780
Yes.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.120
In these 30 years, have you noticed any change in their engagement, in any way?

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:40.860
Yes.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.880
In general, society's engagement has ...

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.940
... become greater.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.220
In the beginning, people didn't even want to listen to what had happened.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.540
And one has to make people understand ...

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.660
... that things can happen.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.000
Impossible things happen.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.000
We didn't even think that things would happen.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.520
When we saw a clip of him screaming ...

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.620
... my mom said that he was just a crazy person ...

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:16.140
... in Germany. And Germany is so far away, so don't worry.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:21.480
Yes, do you remember hearing him on the radio?

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:22.320
Yes.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.940
You lived in Romania at that time.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.020
Yes.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.820
So is that your first memory of Hitler, hearing him on the radio?

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:30.900
Yes.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:38.740
And you were living peacefully and untouched by ...

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:39.880
Yes.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:44.320
We lived in a small town, that consisted of ...

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.000
... 30.000 inhabitants.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.560
And different kinds of minorities.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.680
And seemingly people were living ...

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.760
... side by side.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:00.920
But ...

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.040
Antisemitism existed.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.180
But you ...

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.640
You hear Hitler on the radio and then ...

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:13.380
... time passes, and things develop ...

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.800
... when you look back, quite fast.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:17.420
Yes.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.840
But still, it's like ...

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.420
A day passes at a time, and suddenly you're living in a ghetto.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:34.380
And then one day, you and your sister, your mother and your father find yourself sitting on a train to Auschwitz.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:35.160
Yes.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.700
It's difficult to understand ...

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.300
When one knows what happened ...

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.160
... it's difficult to understand how that could happen.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.120
It is difficult to understand.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.020
But people are constructed in a way ...

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.800
... that as long as ...

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.480
... life isn't at risk ...

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.380
... you continue.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.400
It worsens.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.920
It's not good.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.300
But ...

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:05.980
You can live with it.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:07.280
It might pass.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.620
I usually say that ...

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.740
An injustice is like a grain of sand in your hand ...

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.280
You can't feel the weight of it.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.180
But injustices have the tendency to ...

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.180
... multiply themselves.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.660
And they become more and more and more.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.840
And in the end, you can't bear them.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.820
That's why it's important to act while there's still time.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.680
And if one compares then to the present ...

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.240
... there are so many similarities ...

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:39.400
... that people should think again both once and twice.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:47.720
You arrived in Auschwitz with your mother, father and sister ...

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:54.300
... what is your first memory from disembarking the train there?

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.400
Powerful floodlights that blind us.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:07.680
A smell that penetrates your body and soul. You don't know what it is.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.100
Mom is crying «they're going to kill us».

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:17.420
«No, this is just the factories where we're going to work», I say to comfort her.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.880
A German with a baton ...

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.360
... is standing there ...

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.960
... deciding the fate ...

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.680
... of those who have arrived.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.660
He points at my mother.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.440
«To the right.»

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:40.900
My sister and I, «to the left».

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.160
«Those are my children», my mother says.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.580
«We want to stay together!»

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.740
«You'll meet them later, you're traveling with a truck and they can walk.»

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:59.420
Water. We didn't get water for three days.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.820
«You'll get coffee when you get there.»

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.920
When they got there ...

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.140
... they turned on the gas taps.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.480
Those are my first memories.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:18.740
And the German you met there, you've later learned that that was Dr. Mengele.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:19.880
Yes.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.900
Who was one of the worst, we could say.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:24.720
Yes.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:33.500
How did you experience the solidarity between the prisoners, you you trust the others?

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.800
... Or was it just your sister that you felt you could ..?

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:41.800
It was my sister and ...

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.760
... our group of friends. Those of us who had come from the same town.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:52.560
There was small cliques.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.800
And the solidarity there wasn't greater ...

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.540
... than ...

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.380
... it is in the world in general.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:03.380
People weren't better ...

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:05.140
They weren't worse.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.480
They were exactly like ...

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.160
... they are in the world today.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:12.100
Everywhere.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.440
You sister, who is still alive ...

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:19.800
... and whom you talk with every day ...

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:26.720
... how significant was she to you in Auschwitz?

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:32.200
If we hadn't been together, we might not have survived.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:36.680
Because if one of us felt completely defeated ...

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:38.600
... the other could ...

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:40.400
... pep her up.

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:42.520
And the other way around.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.680
So we tried to ...

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.820
... help each other endure this.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.460
When you throughout these years ...

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:56.800
... have spoken to young people and told the story ...

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:02.520
What has been the hardest thing for you to tell and to relive?

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:08.480
The hardest ... Everything is hard.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:11.240
Every time I tell the story, I'm back there.

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:14.380
And every time, there is a process.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:16.540
Because this is impossible to forget.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.900
But you can learn to live with it.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.760
And to achieve that ...

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.760
... you need to ...

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.340
... process it again and again and again.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.080
Are there things ...

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.940
... when you're telling this story, that you ...

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.300
... don't tell?

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.000
That are too difficult to talk about?

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.760
There is nothing I don't talk about.

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.700
And there are no questions I won't answer.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.840
When I'm out in the schools, I usually tell them to ask questions ...

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:54.760
And ..

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:58.400
The questions are more important than the answers.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:00.640
And ...

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.740
There are no ...

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:08.140
... questions that are forbidden.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:11.760
I want them to be able to ask any questions.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:16.820
You were ...

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.640
You were in another camp when you were freed.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:26.800
Yes. I was at Auschwitz first.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.180
And from there I went to ...

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:32.420
... three different work camps.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:37.540
And from there, in the last stage of the war ...

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:39.260
... to Bergen-Belsen.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:40.860
And your sister was still with you?

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.240
Yes, she was there.

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:46.340
How were you at that time?

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.220
What state were you in?

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.360
I became sick in Bergen-Belsen.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.400
First, I was ...

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:00.220
I was infected with lice.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:04.220
I had managed to stay away from the lice the whole time.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.540
But in Bergen-Belsen, I was infected.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.860
And I was quite sick for a while.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.760
And there, she was the one who helped me back to life. So ...

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.740
We have always been there for each other.

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:22.360
And later I got jaundice.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:25.060
And ...

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:27.680
... later I recovered from that as well.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:35.320
And later the Swedish Red Cross came ...

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:42.340
And they were bringing, to Sweden, 10.000 survivors ...

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:45.920
... and sick people from the hospital ...

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.720
... and their relatives.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:52.200
What is your strongest memory from arriving in Sweden?

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.320
It's milk chocolate!

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:07.020
When we arrived, the Swedish Women's Voluntary Defence Service were at the dock ...

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:11.480
... and they gave us hot chocolate and sandwiches.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:15.540
And the drink was divine!

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.520
Sweet and hot.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.400
So milk chocolate represents Sweden even today.

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:27.540
I just have to say that ...

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.040
The memory you talk about ...

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:31.800
And I just have to thank you for letting me listen to your story ...

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:34.820
I consider you a herald.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.620
And when you talk about the grain of sand that doesn't weigh anything, the injustice ...

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:42.980
And when they become more, you can feel the weight ...

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:44.900
What you say in this sentence ...

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:50.620
... is wiser than anything Adolf Hitler managed to do as a screaming demagogue.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:54.000
And it is a privilege to be able so sit and listen to you.

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:08.800
Your life turned out well, you got married and had three kids ...

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.400
You became a psychologist ...

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:16.500
Other survivors have said that they have a ...

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:21.260
... special relationship with food, after having ...

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:22.200
... been so hungry.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:23.080
Oh yes.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:26.820
You either have a fully stocked refrigerator ...

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.720
... or you have an empty refrigerator.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:31.980
And mine is empty.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:33.620
Why is that?

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:35.840
Because I cannot throw food away.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:38.240
And one person doesn't eat that much.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:43.400
To buy food and then to let it waste away ...

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:47.260
... that is the worst thing I can imagine.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.500
To throw away a small piece of bread ...

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:51.520
... is impossible.

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:56.120
When it gets moldy, I will look at it and get sad ...

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:01.040
And I don't quite know what to do with it.

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.280
It's not easy.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:05.900
Have you had nightmares?

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:08.060
If I've had nightmares?

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:09.160
Yes.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:11.280
I've had nightmares.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:12.320
What ...

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:15.100
What kind of things might appear in your nightmares?

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:18.940
In the beginning, the nightmares were that I was in Sweden ...

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:23.120
... and that I travel back to Romania ...

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:26.860
... and that my children are stuck in Sweden and that I can't meet them.

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:28.820
Because they keep me there.

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:31.200
And ...

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:35.000
And that ...

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:38.900
I didn't want to go back, but I did.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:42.340
And when I got back home ...

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.080
... these nightmares disappeared.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:49.280
Well, not completely, but they weren't as intensive.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:55.080
And then when I went back to Auschwitz it was about the same ...

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:57.280
It became easier.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:07.140
With the background that you have ...

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:08.920
What ...

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:11.300
What does the world look like in your eyes?

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:14.300
Sad.

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:20.960
Like I mentioned, in some way, I see a ...

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:22.560
Déjà vu.

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:26.720
I have a feeling that I'm looking through binoculars ...

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:28.300
... the wrong way.

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:32.040
What I see is small.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:34.400
The things that I once saw big.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:36.580
And I wonder ...

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.720
... what is it going to become?

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:41.260
Are things going to develop the same way?

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:43.940
That can't happen.

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:45.940
That ...

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:48.700
That is what I try to ...

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:51.700
... say, that there are so many ...

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:53.700
similarities.

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:59.840
And it could happen, maybe not the same way, but when they say that ...

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:01.960
... that ...

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:09.880
... that everything is narration, the postmodernists, for example ...

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:12.860
What do they mean?

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:16.780
That what is true has become a lie?

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:20.860
That Auschwitz never existed?

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:22.800
Is that true?

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.880
So the ...

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.440
The world has become strange.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:32.400
But it is still possible to resist.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:35.460
Because, I might have said this earlier ...

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:39.660
It looks so terrifying, but we cannot be scared.

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:42.900
As I've said, to take the bull by the horns. Fight against it.

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.960
And I feel that the young people are with me today!

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:48.580
People ask: «What are we going to do?».

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:50.880
«What kind of possibilities do we have?»

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:55.940
Unfortunately, there are no short term possibilities.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:00.500
But long term, the solution is education.

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:05.100
We can see how education has already ...

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:10.140
... brought out a youth that ...

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:12.640
... is more active ...

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:16.680
... who are there for each other, who are less egoistical ...

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:18.600
... who have more empathy ...

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:21.080
And ...

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:25.320
... who hopefully have a moral compass.

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:31.080
In this show, we have earlier ...

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:33.180
... talked about faith and doubt.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:34.060
Yes.

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:39.720
And I know that you still have your faith from your childhood.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:44.580
And a final question, do you still believe in God?

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:45.840
My ...

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:50.060
Every Jew has his or her own faith.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:57.460
And my faith hasn't changed. I'm a Jew, and I will stay a Jew.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:15:59.540
And ...

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:03.740
And I have never been a believer who takes everything literal.

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:08.360
But the faith that I had, I still have, yes.

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:12.640
Hédi Fried, thank you so much for being here with us tonight.

